humans have been altering plants and  animals for thousands of years we  started with a process called selective  breeding where organisms that express  desired traits are bred together to pass  on those traits to their offspring the  result of this breeding is all around  you for example early farmers bred for  larger and tastier corn by selectively  planting only corn kernels that came  from corn that was larger and tastier as  humans learned more about the role of  genes and DNA and trait expression we  started developing new techniques for  alterations GMOs or genetically modified  organisms are created by introducing a  gene from another organism or a gene  that has been modified in a lab for  example biotechnologists have created  varieties of corn that are resistant to  the herbicide roundup by introducing a  modified version of a corn gene recently  scientists have developed a whole new  way to alter DNA without introducing new  or modified genes that tool is called  CRISPR cast nine now scientists can  alter corn to be resistant to Roundup by  simply changing the existing genetic  makeup of corn this is much more  efficient and precise than the method of  introducing transgenes  so what is CRISPR cast 9 kes 9 is a  protein that comes from bacteria like  streptococcus pyogenes and functions as  a defense against viruses a virus  invades of bacterium by inserting its  genetic material into the bacterium DNA  to make more copies of itself when  infected with a virus the bacterium  stores a small piece of viral DNA so  that the next time the virus attacks it  can produce an RNA copy which will guide  cast 9 to the invading viral DNA  Kathleen then makes a single  double-stranded cut in the viral genome  learning from streptococcus scientists  have been able to take advantage of this  precise defense mechanism to create  specific edits to  genome let's see how this would work if  scientists wanted to change a mouse's  fur color from black to white scientists  can provide cast name with an RNA  complement containing the gene they want  to modify so that cassadine knows what  DNA to look for here our scientist  provides cast 9 with the RNA complement  of the black for a color gene and  inserts it into a mouse embryo cast 9  that makes a single cuts in the gene and  scientists can make a desired mutation  in one of two ways the first way is just  to hope that the Edit you want occurs by  chance this works surprisingly well the  cut ends of the DNA are fairly unstable  and accidental insertions deletions and  replacements are common scientists  perform CRISPR in a large number of  organisms and then screened the  organisms for the desired  characteristics the second way is to  provide the organism with a template of  the gene with a desired mutation so that  repair enzymes in the organism can use  the template to make exactly the  mutation provided the embryo is placed  back into a mother to finish development  and is born with white fur now that we  understand CRISPR casts 9 we can  envision a myriad of potential  applications gene therapy could be done  in embryos so that babies grow up  without ever experiencing a disease gene  therapy could also potentially be done  in adults through edits to stem cells  which are cells that can develop into  any of the specialized cells in the body  under the right conditions genetic  diseases such as sickle cell and cystic  fibrosis could be prevented researchers  are also experimenting with a new  application of CRISPR to achieve gene  Drive a mechanism that ensures both  chromosomes in an organism received the  same edit so that all of their offspring  and all their offsprings offspring  inherit the mutation with the goal of  forcing that mutation to all members of  a population for example researchers  could eliminate malaria using gene Drive  and mosquitoes  CRISPR is the most powerful gene editing  tool ever seen to date but it is still  in a highly experimental phase there are  no fully developed laws regulating  CRISPR and there are many concerns about  both its practical and moral  consequences as technology continues to  advance researchers lawmakers and the  public will have to decide how to  properly use CRISPR  [Music]  [Music]
 

